 need entries in in vector space or at  least a metric space so it's a bit more  than an implementation problem  a small note categorical values are  discrete anyhow  so if you have categorical categorical  variable a clean categorical variable  the solution is simply to create a  special category  which you call missing and to encode the  missing values in this category  the rest of the talk i'll focus on  missing values  in numerical variables so there's a  classic statistic literature on this i  mean the underlying model  is that we have first a complete data  generating process  second a random process that occludes  the entries  and for a specific missing value  situation  missing at random which is defined as  the probability  of missingness does not depend on the  non-observed value  then there is a very powerful  theoretical reason that tells us that in  this situation in the  missing at random situation we can still  do maximum likelihood  while ignoring the unobserved value and  retrieve a  good maximum likelihood estimate  so there are other variants of missing  values  one which is a subtype of missing at  random which is  missing completely at random missingness  is independent from the data  it's easy the results above still hold  and another one which is missing not at  random  which is we're not under the assumptions  of missing at random  and in such a situation the missingness  is not ignorable so it's a  challenging statistical problem  here are a few observation i'm showing  the complete data the missing completely  at random you've seen that we have only  you know randomly losing data points but  there's no structure in this  and the missing not at random and we see  here that we have  censoring basically above certain  threshold we're starting to lose  information and  we can see why this distorts the data  now there's a big theory  here of statistics but  first there isn't always an observed  value  what's the age of the space of a single  and second  we're not trying to maximize likelihoods  we're trying to do machine learning and  prediction  so this theory doesn't completely apply  to machine learning  yet the bloody machine learning toolkit  still doesn't work  what can we do about this the first  thing we can do  is imputation we can replace the missing  values by  likely values that they may take  so there's a large statistical  literature here  but they're really focused on in sample  settings  what about machine learning how about  completing the test set with the train  set what to do with  the prediction target why  so in socket learn we have a few  imputation procedures that will work out  of sample  the mean imputation which is a special  case of univariate imputation  so replacing the missing value by the  mean of the feature  this can be done by the simple computer  from the classical statistical point of  view this  is really a bad idea because this  distorts the distribution you can see it  here  we've replaced on one feature missing  value by the mean of the feature and it  creates a line that you can see with  your naked eye in the distribution  and standard statistical point of view  of missing value tells us that good  imputation  must preserve data properties used by  the later analysis step  so for this we can do richer imputation  conditional imputation for instance  where we model one feature as a function  of  others the problem is that it's costly  especially on large databases it scales  extremely poorly  one possible implementation is to  iteratively predict one feature  as a function of others like in the  famous mice software and in scikit-learn  this is done by the iterative computer  so we did a bit of theory we  we looked at this problem from a  theoretical standpoint  and so in the paper by julie josetal  and our recommendations are that we  found that they're basically different  regimes  in statistics typically you prefer the  conditional  imputation because it's richer however  in machine learning we showed that mean  and petition is typically  sufficient if you use a powerful  non-linear learner  because the nonlinear learner will  basically detect the irregularities like  the  line that i showed you before and will  predict very well from these  now there's another pooch that works  only with  trees but trees are very good  to deal with data that has a  discrete or semi-discrete topology  and so you can adapt the trees to work  directly with the missing values  so you can learn trees that make the  decision  on whether the feature takes certain  values  including missing value and so it  readily learns to handle missing values  this in psychic learn is exposed in the  hist gradient boosting classifier  and the regressor and so what we see  empirically  is that this approach the handling  missing values inside trees  works extremely well and that if you  have enough data  mean imputation also works well  now imputation may not be enough  for instance consider the pathological  case where y  depends only on whether the data is  missing or not  for instance in tax fraud detection  if you impute perfectly this data you  break the  the prediction it makes the prediction  impossible the solution is to  add a missingness indicator so if you  take a computer  you use adding indicator equals true  now if we go back to simulations  what we see is that if we're imputing  this indicator is necessary for the  iterative  imputer but it's not for the mean  in pewter and  using the supportive missing values  inside the trees  works very well and does not need this  extra  indicator so the takeoff message  is that practically using  the native support of missing values in  trees  works best and if we do imputation  when we have missing not at random  situations we really need to add an  indicator  and in this situation iterative  imputation  can be detrimental compared to mean  imputation  so to summarize how do we learn on dirty  tables  without cleaning we will prepare the  data with a column transformer  and quite often it's best to use the his  gradient boosting  classifier regressor this is a good  default as a learner  if we have dirty categories we can do  statistical modeling of the non-curated  categorical data  by capturing its string forms and so we  can use  similarity encoding or other string  based dirty category encoders  that can be found in the dirty cat  software  if we have missing values  the best option is to use the native  support  in his gradient boosting regressor or  classifier  if we cannot do this quite often it is  enough to use  the mean imputation and possibly to add  a missing value indicator  this is ongoing research and you can  find more  on the dirty data project  thank you
 

